WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.942
Welcome to UFI Career Studio. Think clearly in

00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:07.883
a technical interview. Make your reasoning visible by

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:11.825
clarifying the problem, testing the solution, and discussing

00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:15.767
tradeoffs. We will work through five practical steps,

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:19.708
with examples you can adapt to your own

00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:20.201
experience.

00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:25.271
Before solving, restate the problem in your own

00:00:25.271 --> 00:00:29.459
words. Confirm the expected inputs and outputs, important

00:00:29.459 --> 00:00:33.646
constraints, and what should happen in unusual cases.

00:00:33.646 --> 00:00:37.834
If something is unclear, ask rather than guessing

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:42.022
silently. In a coding exercise, consider empty input

00:00:42.022 --> 00:00:46.209
and duplicates. In a systems discussion, ask about

00:00:46.209 --> 00:00:50.397
users, scale, and reliability. Clarification is part of

00:00:50.397 --> 00:00:53.538
solving the problem, not a delay.

00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:57.800
Describe a simple workable approach before adding complexity.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.184
Walk through a small example so the interviewer

00:01:01.184 --> 00:01:04.568
can follow your reasoning. State any assumption you

00:01:04.568 --> 00:01:07.952
need to make and explain why the approach

00:01:07.952 --> 00:01:11.335
should work. A clear baseline gives you something

00:01:11.335 --> 00:01:14.719
to test and improve. Jumping straight to an

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:18.103
elaborate solution makes it harder to spot mistakes

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:19.372
or discuss alternatives.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.655
Test the solution as you develop it. Use

00:01:23.655 --> 00:01:27.061
a normal example, an edge case, and a

00:01:27.061 --> 00:01:30.466
case that could expose a wrong assumption. Explain

00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:33.871
what you expect before checking the result. If

00:01:33.871 --> 00:01:37.276
a test fails, trace the behavior instead of

00:01:37.276 --> 00:01:40.682
changing several things at once. This demonstrates a

00:01:40.682 --> 00:01:44.087
disciplined process and helps you recover without losing

00:01:44.087 --> 00:01:46.215
track of the original problem.

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:50.693
Discuss tradeoffs in terms of the actual requirements.

00:01:50.693 --> 00:01:54.303
An approach may use less memory but be

00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:57.912
harder to maintain, or run faster but introduce

00:01:57.912 --> 00:02:01.522
operational complexity. Explain which factor matters most here

00:02:01.522 --> 00:02:05.132
and why. Do not describe a solution as

00:02:05.132 --> 00:02:08.741
universally best. A strong technical conversation shows that

00:02:08.741 --> 00:02:12.351
you can make reasonable choices under constraints and

00:02:12.351 --> 00:02:15.058
revise them when the requirements change.

00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:19.831
If you get stuck, summarize what you know

00:02:19.831 --> 00:02:23.745
and identify the smallest unresolved question. Try a

00:02:23.745 --> 00:02:27.659
simpler example, draw the data flow, or ask

00:02:27.659 --> 00:02:31.573
for clarification. Keep the interviewer informed without narrating

00:02:31.573 --> 00:02:35.487
every keystroke. For practice, solve a modest problem

00:02:35.487 --> 00:02:39.401
aloud and include your tests. Afterwards, review whether

00:02:39.401 --> 00:02:43.315
your explanation made both the solution and the

00:02:43.315 --> 00:02:45.272
reasoning easy to follow.
